Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A change in some exogenous variable may affect both demand and supply":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A change in some exogenous variable may affect both the demand and supply in some market, and thus, affect the quantity demanded and quantity supplied, and thereby the price.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the core concepts in comparative statics, and it underlies how exogenous factors shape endogenous variables in a market.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Factors that are outside of the control of some model/market may affect the supply and demand. For example:</p><ul><li>A change in consumer tastes &amp; preferences</li><li>A change in input costs</li><li>A change in consumer income</li><li>A change in exchange rates</li><li>A change in government policy/new subsidies/taxes</li><li>Etc.</li></ul><p>all may shift the demand and supply curves, thus resulting in changes in price and quantity demanded/supplied at the equilibrium.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect\">A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economists tend to overestimate the detrimental effect of tariffs\">Economists tend to overestimate the detrimental effect of tariffs</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The effects of capital deepening are uncertain\">The effects of capital deepening are uncertain</a></li></ul>","A regime should prevent its defining style of organization from being pushed too far":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A regime should not intensify its defining principle, since that would only worsen the regime and eventually prevent it from functioning at all.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a guideline that Aristotle provides when it comes to reforming regimes.</p><h4>Example</h4><p>For instance, a democracy should not try to reform itself to be even more democratic when it is already democratic, since doing so would cause the regime to lose its moderateness, and thus highlight the negative aspects of any type of regime, and eventually bring about its downfall.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>Its always best to strive for the mean, and not one of the extremes.</li></ul></li></ul>","Activist organizations often shift priorities in order to self-perpetuate":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Often times, activist organizations will switch focus after their initial stated goals have been competed in order to continue functioning. This often leads such groups to be detrimental overall to society, rather than helping society.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that often times, these organizations can stray away from their original goals and support causes that may actually be harmful.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is known as the “March of Dimes” syndrome. Many activist groups in history, once they have successfully achieved their goals, shifted to oftentimes even counterproductive goals in order to continue operating.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li><strong>The March of Dimes itself</strong> — Founded to combat polio, it achieved its goal through vaccines in the 1950s but kept going by shifting its mission to preventing birth defects.</li><li><strong>Antiwar activists turning antinuclear</strong> — Veterans of the Vietnam War protest movement pivoted to leading the anti-nuclear power movement after the Three Mile Island accident in 1979, with figures like Jane Fonda and Tom Hayden at the forefront.</li><li><strong>The LGBTQ+ rights movement</strong> — After achieving major wins (decriminalization of homosexuality, same-sex marriage legalization in 2015, civil rights protections in 2020), activists declared a \"national state of emergency\" in 2023 and pushed for a range of new and more controversial causes.</li><li><strong>The civil rights movement / NAACP</strong> — After landmark legislative victories (Civil Rights Act of 1964, Voting Rights Act of 1965), the movement shifted from demanding equality to demanding affirmative action, reparations, and other causes that contradicted its original integration-focused mission.</li><li><strong>The Southern Poverty Law Center (SPLC)</strong> — Launched to represent individual discrimination victims, it pivoted to suing the KKK, then to publishing expanding lists of \"hate groups\" that critics say include mainstream conservative organizations, all as a fund-raising strategy.</li><li><strong>Black Lives Matter</strong> — Emerged and grew into a massive movement despite the rarity of the specific type of deaths it ostensibly addressed, and critics argue its antipolice posture contributed to more Black deaths via the \"Ferguson Effect.\"</li><li><strong>The #MeToo movement</strong> — Rose to prominence during a period when rates of sexual violence had actually fallen nearly 60% (from 1995 to 2010), and came after corporate America had already instituted strict harassment protections.</li><li><strong>Antismoking activists opposing vaping</strong> — After smoking rates dropped sharply thanks to nicotine vaping, antismoking groups like the Campaign for Tobacco-Free Kids manufactured scares about vaping to maintain relevance and funding, despite vaping being widely recognized as far less harmful than tobacco.</li><li><strong>Environmental/climate activists</strong> — After previous doomsday predictions (overpopulation, energy scarcity, a synthetic-chemical \"cancer epidemic\") proved false, green activists escalated to claiming humanity faces an \"existential threat\" from climate change.</li><li><strong>Jane Fonda specifically</strong> — Highlighted as a special case (\"the March of Jane syndrome\"): she helped kill the nuclear power industry through activism, which caused a switch to coal and increased carbon emissions — and then became a leading climate activist fighting the very problem her earlier activism helped create.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","GDP (per capita) is an inaccurate predictor of wellbeing":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>GDP or GDP per capita is a poor predictor of the level of wellbeing in an economy, since it cannot take into account things like inequality or other seemingly non-quantifiable characteristics of wellbeing (aside from monetary success).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common claim made often by economists and non-economists alike.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>It’s been shown, however, that high GDP correlates relatively highly with non-quantifiable factors such as high happiness or low loneliness.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Authoritarian &amp; non-democratic nations likely overstate their GDP growth\">Authoritarian &amp; non-democratic nations likely overstate their GDP growth</a><ul><li>This is perhaps a possible distorting factor when it comes to measuring such correlation? However, I would imagine that in such countries, measurements for happiness may also be overstated.</li></ul></li></ul>","Good institutions are the source of economic growth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Good institutions are the source of economic growth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>If this were true, then we would want to ensure that are institutions are good as possible in order to maximize economic development.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of ‘good institutions’ is either tautological or unsupported\">The concept of ‘good institutions’ is either tautological or unsupported</a><ul><li>This note refutes the stuff set out in this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Mainstream economics’ need for assumptions in their models justifies government intervention into the economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Mainstream economics makes many claims about the assumptions which must be true in order for its economic models to be applied and thus the economy to be managed. Consequently, using the fact that their models typically fail to be satisfied, they justify state intervention into the economy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an implicit argument against mainstream economics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The critique is essentially that mainstream economic is focusing too much on models and math, and failing to see that they are too focused on their models and not enough on the actual behavior of people and the applicability of their models. In fact, Vernon Smith demonstrated that many of the typically assumptions in mainstream economics are in fact unnecessary.</p><h4>References</h4>","Moral mastery is achieved through choice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Moral mastery, the ability to choose when one is faced with good and evil, isn’t determined when one is born. It is something that one develops through time or otherwise acquires by choice.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common idea in philosophy and literature.</p><h4><em>East of Eden</em></h4><p>The idea of <em>timshel</em> or “Thou Mayest” is present throughout the book and it is something that the characters consistently struggle with.</p><ul><li>Adam struggles with the ability to overcome his love for Cathy and love on, until he finally confronts her and stands his ground against her.</li><li>This is an idea that Samuel Hamilton espouses during his discussion of naming Adam’s children.<ul><li>He says that we are not determined by our birth, and we are able to shape who we become.</li></ul></li><li>Lee also goes through this process, abandoning her use of pidgin as a means of protecting himself and actively beings to help the Trask family.<ul><li>He reveals intimate details about his family past in order to help Adam embrace <em>timshel</em>.</li></ul></li></ul><h4><em>The Brothers Karamzov</em></h4><p>The idea of “active love” that is expressed in this book fits in with the philosophy of the importance of individual choice.</p><ul><li>One ought not to simply fall into sin and debauchery, as Fyodor does.</li><li>Although the Karamazovs are born with the Karamazov spirit, they must be able to conquer it and not fall entirely under its influence.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it\">An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it</a><ul><li>Aristotle argues that in order for someone to be responsible for something (and thus be considered virtuous for it or just praised for it), they must have choose it. In this way, his idea of virtue connects with the idea from this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Although there are some crucial industries that require patents to generate new knowledge, such as pharmaceuticals or chemicals. Beyond these industries, however, it seems that patents are usually not the main driver of innovation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial part of the argument made by Chang in his book, as this increase in patent prevalence is dragging innovation.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research\">Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas\">Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas</a></li></ul>","Neo-mercantilism is the modern adaptation of historical mercantilism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Whereas historical mercantilism had a single-minded focus on maintaining a trade surplus and accumulating precious materials, neo-mercantilism is more complex, as it integrates capitalistic elements.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important distinct to make, since many countries today are considered to be “neo-mercantilist,” though this does not mean that they are mercantilist in the 16-17th century sense.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p><strong>Neo-mercantilism:</strong></p><ul><li>Focuses on national economic strength.</li><li>Favoring exports over imports, though with nuance.</li><li>State-guided capitalism.</li><li>Focus on industrial policy; technology, infrastructure, and innovation are prioritized.</li><li>State-subsidized/directed industries (which are considered to be crucial for the state).<ul><li>Low domestic competition.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Mercantilism:</strong></p><ul><li>Resource extraction from colonies that also serve as markets for the mother state.</li><li>Heavy economic regulation and government monopolies.</li><li>Hoarding of precious mineral wealth.</li><li>Maximize export and minimize imports.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom\">Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom</a></li></ul>","New nucleotides are added to the 3' end of a DNA or RNA":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When new nucleotides are added to a DNA or RNA, it is added to the 3’ end of the chain of nucleotides. Thus, DNA or RNA is always read from the 5’ to the 3’ end.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This information might appear on an AP Bio test. This is also analogous to how New amino acids are added to the C-terminus on a polypeptide chain.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"New amino acids are added to the C-terminus on a polypeptide chain\">New amino acids are added to the C-terminus on a polypeptide chain</a></li></ul>","State dissolution often begins with devolution of power":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Often, before a state collapses, power becomes more and more decentralized within the state.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This seems to be a common thread with states that collapse, namely, that more and more power is devolved to local areas or specific people who are not necessarily loyal to the state.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>In crushing the Taiping Rebellion, the Qing state devolved power to local commanders, such as Zeng Guofan and his Hunan Army.</li><li>Near the end of the Roman Republic, the loyalty of troops were much more towards their commanders, than towards the state, since their commanders were the ones who directly paid them.</li><li>During the end of the Eastern Zhou, specifically through the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods, more power was diverted to local lords, eventually leading to the end of the Zhou and the establishment of the Qin.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The city is the fundamental political unit":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The very fundamental political unit is the city, and it comes before considerations regarding things like the household, since the household is like an appendage of the city, and without the body (the city), the appendages cannot survive.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explores the importance of the city, since it is the most fundamental political unit.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Things like justice, which require political awareness, are only possible in a city.</p><h4>References</h4>","The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The difference principle is fundamentally an expression of the idea that the natural talents that we are born with (natural contingencies) ought to be shared in society, such that they are used to achieve the greatest common good, which means aiding the worst-off.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This highlights a difference between the difference principle and the principle of redress. While the latter focuses on desert, the former focuses more on achieving the optimizing the expectations of the least advantaged.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>See another note that talks about the nature of the difference principle.</li></ul></li></ul>","The difference principle expresses reciprocity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The difference principle expresses a social condition that is similar to reciprocity, i.e., a principle of mutual benefit.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This ensures that the difference principle is publicly justifiable to everyone.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Given that there are two individuals, $A$ and $B$, where $A$ is better-off than $B$,</p><ol><li>$B$ accepts being worse-off than $A$ since the difference principle ensures that $A$ being better-off helps him the most.</li><li>$A$ accepts helping $B$ since <ol><li>A’s being better-off is a result of the social institutions which exist, which $B$ is a part of, and </li><li>the idea that $A$ deserves his position presupposes a cooperative scheme rather than just justify it. One cannot appeal to desert to resist the difference principle, because desert only operates after the basic structure is already justified.</li></ol></li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses fraternity\">The difference principle expresses fraternity</a><ul><li>Another argument for the difference principle.</li></ul></li></ul>","The ills usually blamed on private property come from vice, not from ownership":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Having private property does not necessarily make people greedy or corrupt.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Leftists often claim that becoming private property corrupts people, and this note refutes that claim.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle argues that corruption is not a necessary result of having private property, corruption if most often simply a result of vice. On the other hand, having a lot of wealth (unlimited acquisition) can be corrupting influence that make people stray from virtue.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health\">Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health</a><ul><li>Perhaps this provides some empirical evidence in support of this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","The problem of evil can be answered by appeal to the limits of human understanding":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One response to the existence of evil is to deny that we are positioned to judge: the apparent gratuitousness of suffering is a fact about the limits of human comprehension rather than about the world, so no inference from evil to the non-existence of God goes through.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the most common defence against the problem of evil, and it is also the least falsifiable, which is both its strength and the standard objection to it.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The Book of Job gives the argument its canonical form. God’s answer from the whirlwind is not a justification of Job’s suffering but a demonstration of the gulf between what Job can grasp and what is going on.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The amount of evil in the world makes the existence of a perfectly loving God unlikely\">The amount of evil in the world makes the existence of a perfectly loving God unlikely</a><ul><li>The argument this is a reply to.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One should not attempt to analyze faith with logical arguments\">One should not attempt to analyze faith with logical arguments</a><ul><li>The more general position this defence tends to collapse into.</li></ul></li></ul>","The real-world applicability of the Pigou effect is contested":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Pigou effect, which states that a general decrease in prices leads to an increase in real wealth, leading to increased aggregate consumption, seems to hold theoretical plausibility, though its real-world applicability is contested.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The lack of the inclusion of the Pigou effect is one of the critiques of Keynes’s <em>General Theory</em>. The theory also seems to lack support from empirical evidence.</p><h4>Why?</h4><ul><li>The Pigou effect’s claim that deflation leads to increased real wealth is undermined by the fact that such deflation also increase the value of debts, which can counteract its effect in increasing wealth.</li><li>In Japan, its sustained deflationary crisis was not solved through the Pigou effect, as during the liquidity crisis, people hoarded their money instead of spending it, as the increased real wealth suggested by the Pigou effect would imply that they would have.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect\">A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect</a><ul><li>Defines the mechanism at issue. The Pigou effect is the Real Wealth Effect described there.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
